/-- Finite polygon reduction up to the final bijective renaming of edge coordinates.
The initial triangle quotient homeomorphism is given. The conclusion stops just
before the final renaming in Step 12; `labelRenaming_realized` realizes that last
operation and preserves the boundary-loop images. The geometric quotient comparison
in Step 1 is supplied by the given H₀ and its exact parameter certificate.

Source: Gallier–Xu, author version 8 January 2025, Definition 6.2, pp.81–83; Definition 6.3,
pp.84–86, inverse rules p.92, formal invariants pp.87–89; Lemma 6.1 six-step reduction pp.92–95,
normal words pp.88–90, Theorem 6.1 p.96; Proposition 3.2 and its prescribed-boundary remark p.31,
Note F.4 p.167; Proposition 3.4 and Note F.6 p.168. Lee, Introduction to Topological Manifolds,
second edition 2011, Proposition 6.14 p.173 and classification Step 1 p.175. Seifert–Threlfall, A
Textbook of Topology, Goldman translation 1980, §37 p.138 and §38 Step 2 p.140, Figures 59–61. The
chosen exceptional short/empty operations are the finite proper source compositions spelled out
below; no universal improper primitive, p.86 exercise, or optional endpoint-only classification is
assumed.

The input is the original finite abstract simplicial complex on its complete finite vertex set.
Purity excludes extraneous lower-dimensional pieces; the incidence and link conditions are literal
finite simplex conditions. `B` lists exactly the disjoint original boundary cycles. The given
triangle orderings express coherent orientation through the opposite-sign incidence predicate; they
supply no normal-form word or topological conclusion. The barycentric realization retains its
ordinary coordinate subtype topology. Every elementary realization respects the SAME interval
parameters, and the final homeomorphism is the actual composite, including only harmless cyclic face
coordinates and a bijective final relabeling. Euler characteristic is an INTEGER equality.

Complete current natural-language proof of the DAG node:

1. Choose the given coherent orientation of every ORIGINAL triangle, and assign each unoriented edge
its two distinct signed darts a,a⁻¹. Keep face INSTANCES and all positions in each triangle
boundary; repeated occurrences never become different face instances. Let P be the topological
disjoint coproduct of these finitely many closed original triangles, each parametrized by its
standard barycentric simplex. Let Q be the quotient of P by the EQUIVALENCE RELATION GENERATED by
identifying the two occurrences of each paired edge through their SAME normalized interval
parameters. A boundary edge with one occurrence is unpaired. Every triangle has its actual three
corners before quotienting. Opposite induced directions on a paired edge give the signed
identification a with a⁻¹, with endpoints paired by the corresponding interval parameters, not by a
new face or endpoint label convention.

Follow the two-map comparison written in Lee, Introduction to Topological Manifolds, second
edition2011, Proposition6.14 printed p173/PDF192. Use the SAME P with q_K:P→|K|, sending each
triangle by its original barycentric realization, and q_Q:P→Q, the generated edge quotient
projection. Here K is already GIVEN; Lee's surface-triangulation theorem and closed-surface
classification are not used to obtain it. To expose the first quotient-map guard, realize |K| in
ℝ^(V(K)) by standard finite vertex coordinates, so it is a Hausdorff subspace of that
finite-dimensional real coordinate space. Each summand triangle is compact by
topo.compact_standard_simplex_g8. Its tagged image in P is compact by
mathlib.isCompact_image_of_continuousOn, using the continuous coproduct inclusion. The finitely many
tagged images cover P; mathlib.setFinite_isCompact_biUnion proves IsCompact univ, and
mathlib.isCompact_univ_iff_g8 gives CompactSpace P for this SAME coproduct topology. Each summand
q_K is affine in its original vertex coordinates and continuous by the existing affine-continuity
result; topo.continuous_sigma_g8 assembles their continuous coproduct map. Purity means every
simplex of K is a face of some triangle, so q_K is onto |K|. Thus
mathlib.compact_hausdorff_surjection_quotient_g8 makes this original continuous surjection an
ordinary quotient map. This compact/Hausdorff argument is used ONLY for q_K, never to assume Q
Hausdorff.

As in Lee's written comparison, the two maps make exactly the same identifications in triangle
interiors and edge interiors: distinct original simplex interiors have disjoint barycentric
supports, and shared edges are identified by their original interval points. Both identify vertices
only with vertices. It remains to verify Lee's edge-forced VERTEX identification on the GIVEN links,
including boundary vertices. Fix a geometric vertex v. Vertices of its simplicial link correspond to
original edges containing v; link EDGES correspond to original triangles containing v. Because this
link is a finite simplicial circle or interval, its edges can be arranged in one finite cyclic or
linear fan in which consecutive link edges share a link vertex. Their corresponding triangles share
the actual original edge containing v. Pairing that shared edge therefore identifies the two copies
of v. Proceeding along the fan identifies ALL triangle copies of v; at an interval end the boundary
edge is unpaired, but the intervening paired edges still connect the whole fan. Every generating
edge identification preserves the original geometric vertex, so it never identifies copies of
distinct v. Thus q_K(p)=q_K(p′) if and only if q_Q(p)=q_Q(p′), for all p,p′∈P. The fan uses the
stated link hypothesis in place of Lee's punctured-ℝ²/manifold argument; neither an added
closed-surface premise nor a separate triangulation existence theorem is invoked.

Construct the actual inverse continuous maps, rather than a compact-to-Hausdorff homeomorphism
shortcut for Q. Since q_Q is continuous and constant on each q_K fibre by the preceding equality,
mathlib.continuous_map_quotient_fibre_descent_g8 descends it to F:|K|→Q with Fq_K=q_Q. Conversely
q_K takes equal values on each literal paired-edge generator. mathlib.eqvGen_rec_g8 propagates this
equality through reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity to the generated setoid. The exact setoid
quotient lift and topo.continuous_quotient_lift_g8 give a continuous G:Q→|K| with Gq_Q=q_K.
Quotient.eq and quotient induction make these representative formulas and their independence
explicit. For each p∈P, GF(q_Kp)=q_Kp and FG(q_Qp)=q_Qp; surjectivity of q_K and
mathlib.setoid_quotient_induction_g8 extend these identities to EVERY point. Hence F and G are
inverse homeomorphisms. No Hausdorffness of Q, choice of vertex-representative topology or unproved
global coproduct quotient theorem is needed.

To match the circular parameter disks used by the UNCHANGED next step, normalize each ORIGINAL
triangle only by the already-written proper-disk construction there, Gallier–Xu
Proposition3.2+Remark p31/PDF41 and NoteF.4 p167/PDF177. In its original real Euclidean triangle
plane, the closed triangle is a bounded closed convex disk with nonempty interior. The existing
gauge normalizer gives a homeomorphism H of that triangle onto the closed round Complex unit disk.
Specify ψ from the parameter circle to the triangle boundary by the three original sides, retaining
their SAME normalized interval parameters and coherent directions; the three finite closed arcs and
their inverse side maps agree at corners, so the retained closed-pair pasting mechanism gives an
actual boundary homeomorphism. Apply the written arbitrary-prescribed-boundary radial extension to
H∘ψ on the round circle and compose with H^−1. This yields a disk-to-ORIGINAL-triangle homeomorphism
extending ψ; invert it for the reverse normalization. Taking the coproduct of these face
homeomorphisms and descending through the SAME paired-edge interval identifications gives the two
inverse quotient maps exactly as in the retained proper-move proof. Thus the circular presentation
used later represents Q with all original boundary parameters unchanged, not a silently substituted
disk shape. Only this already supplied gauge/radial/pasting/quotient mechanism is reused; no Cairns
input, improper cut, new move or short-monogon closure is introduced.

Now express the vertices in the required incoming-dart convention of Gallier–Xu Definition6.2
pp81–83. For each consecutive pair u v in an oriented face boundary, place an undirected corner
relation u~v⁻¹. Its endpoints are the terminal endpoint of u and the initial endpoint of v, which is
the terminal endpoint of v⁻¹. Reversing the face reverses the relation without changing it. The
common signed edge darts already encode the paired-edge endpoint identifications across face
instances; together with the within-triangle corner identifications, these relations generate
precisely the vertex fibres of q_Q just compared with q_K. Therefore the connected components of
this finite corner-relation graph are the geometric quotient vertices. Multiplicity of corner
relations is retained, including distinct occurrences with identical endpoint labels. The GIVEN link
fan yields a cyclic inward list at an inner vertex or an interval inward list with its two
boundary-end darts at a boundary vertex. At the simplicial starting point no consecutive aa⁻¹ string
occurs. This explicitly binds the p83 exercise to the original quotient instead of replacing the
incoming neighbors by successor darts.

For dual connectedness use ONLY the saturated-clopen argument of Lee's classification proof Step1,
printed p175/PDF194. Suppose the dual graph of original faces has more than one component. In P the
union of the triangle summands belonging to one component is open and closed. Its saturation under
q_Q remains the SAME union: a paired edge joins dual-adjacent faces, a boundary edge has no partner,
and the vertex fan proved above puts all copies of each geometric vertex in one dual component.
There is therefore no vertex-crossing between components. By the ordinary quotient topology, its
image and complement are nonempty open-and-closed sets in Q. The homeomorphism |K|≅Q contradicts the
given connectedness. Thus the dual graph is connected; no pasting transformation or later
classification conclusion is used here.

For the vertex-edge graph follow Gallier–Xu Proposition3.4's written proof NoteF.6, printed
p168/PDF178. Fix a vertex v and let V_v be the vertices reachable from v by finite edge paths. If a
simplex touches V_v then all its vertices belong to V_v, since any two distinct vertices of that
simplex span an edge. The complementary vertex set has the same property, so the reachable simplices
and complementary simplices form disjoint subcomplexes. In this finite GIVEN realization, their
preimages under q_K are the two saturated open-and-closed unions of tagged triangle summands; purity
ensures every point is represented and no non-triangle piece is omitted. The quotient map q_K
therefore makes their realizations open and closed. A nonempty complement would disconnect |K|,
contrary to its given connectedness. Every vertex is thus reachable and the finite vertex-edge graph
is connected. This is the direct source reachability argument on the given K, not a
manifold-classification or punctured-plane substitute. This starting comparison/connectivity
component supplies no later move theorem by itself. The later one-surviving-side, empty-slide and
monogon-donor APPLICATIONS are now supplied by the explicitly chosen proper finite source
compositions in Steps3/6/7; the generic p86 exercise is not invoked.

2. P1 replaces each a by bc and each a⁻¹ by c⁻¹b⁻¹, with fresh distinct labels b,c. Its interval
parameter map and inverse are the piecewise linear reparametrizations identifying the two
half-intervals with the original edge. P1⁻¹ is permitted only when (b,c⁻¹) is the FULL two-entry
incoming vertex, so no other incident edge meets the removed subdivision vertex. These maps retain
the parameter identifications on all unaffected edges.

Use Complex as the fixed real normed Euclidean plane throughout: its ordinary Euclidean norm defines
the circular parameter disks, gauge target balls/spheres and the radial source formula. No Fin2→Real
maximum-norm ball is identified with a round disk. For a PROPER P2 write the affected face word UV
with U,V both nonempty. Its cut endpoints are distinct positions in the abstract parameter circle,
even when the global edge quotient identifies them. Draw the straight chord between these positions
in the circular parameter disk. The two closed pieces K₁,K₂ meet exactly in that chord and are the
closures of nonempty bounded convex open half-disks. Distinct circle endpoints ensure that the chord
line passes through the disk interior, so both pieces have nonempty Euclidean interior. Each Kᵢ is
closed, convex and metrically bounded. The existing gauge_convex_body_unit_ball_g10 supplies an
ambient homeomorphism Hᵢ taking Kᵢ onto the closed unit disk and its frontier onto S1. Its closure
clause applies to Kᵢ itself because Kᵢ is closed. No general crosscut, classification or annulus
theorem is used.

Specify a boundary homeomorphism ψᵢ from the new face's parameter circle to ∂Kᵢ: on its U or V arc
retain each original side's interval parameter, and on its fresh d or d⁻¹ arc use the same chord
parameter with the required opposite direction. These two proper closed arcs cover each boundary and
meet only at the two distinct cut endpoints. Their interval maps and inverses agree at those
endpoints. The actual two-closed-set pasting theorem, together with continuity on domain
restrictions, gives continuity of ψᵢ and its inverse; bijectivity follows from the two arc
parametrizations. Thus this is an actual boundary homeomorphism, not just a list of matching edge
names.

Use the NEW written geometric component Gallier–Xu Proposition3.2 and its Remark, printedp31/PDF41,
with the complete radial-extension proof in NoteF.4 printedp167/PDF177. Apply its
arbitrary-prescribed-boundary-homeomorphism extension with C the open unit ball and a=0 to
βᵢ=Hᵢ∘ψᵢ:S1→S1. The exact radial map is Tᵢ(0)=0 and Tᵢ(z)=||z|| • βᵢ((1/||z||) • z) for z≠0 in the
closed unit disk, where both scalar actions are REAL scalar multiplication on Complex. Thus z/||z||
in the source formula means (1/||z||) • z, not an unspecified division in another norm model. By the
existing norm_smul supplier, positive ||z|| gives ||(1/||z||) • z||=1, and for the nonnegative real
radius ρ=||z|| and unit-norm βᵢ value, ||ρ • βᵢ((1/||z||) • z)||=ρ. On the punctured disk the
denominator is nonzero; norm continuity, continuous real division there, continuous scalar
multiplication and composition give continuity away from0. No reciprocal is claimed continuous at0.
Since βᵢ takes its values on S1, ||Tᵢ(z)||=||z||; this is the source's center estimate with M=1 and
proves continuity at0. Radius together with the sphere homeomorphism proves bijectivity. The real
plane is finite-dimensional and proper, hence its closed unit ball is compact; the
compact-set/subtype equivalence supplies CompactSpace of that disk.
Continuous.homeoOfEquivCompactToT2 then upgrades this actual continuous bijection to a homeomorphism
of unit disks. Its T2 target here is only the Euclidean disk subtype. Composing with Hᵢ⁻¹ gives a
disk homeomorphism extending the prescribed ψᵢ. This is the written convex-disk/radial source
construction, with the native gauge normalizer and compact-to-Hausdorff execution explicitly bound;
it supplies no exceptional monogon construction. For P1 and its full-valence-two inverse, the finite
piecewise-linear boundary reparametrization stated above is a circle homeomorphism: the maps on its
finite closed parameter arcs and their inverses agree at endpoints, so repeated closed-pair pasting
gives their continuity. Apply the very same unit-disk F4 radial extension to that boundary
homeomorphism and its inverse on each affected face. Thus the edge subdivision maps also have actual
continuous disk extensions, preserving the parameters on all unaffected sides.

For the inverse P2⁻¹ require TWO DISTINCT face instances AND require each designated d,d⁻¹
occurrence to be a PROPER boundary arc: in the two words Ud and d⁻¹V this means U,V are both
nonempty. Other common edge labels remain external quotient identifications. The prescribed boundary
maps above identify the two donor disks with K₁,K₂ and match their glued chord parameters. The same
construction read in reverse realizes the proper inverse. Distinct faces alone is insufficient when
one donor is a monogon whose whole circle is the glued edge.

Now bind the global realization maps to the literal parameter-disk quotient, whose setoid is
generated by the specified edge-point identifications and endpoint identifications. Each donor-disk
map into the old realization is continuous after composing its piece homeomorphism with the
canonical quotient projection. Assemble these two maps by continuous_sum_elim, and include the
unchanged face maps by the continuous_sigma criterion. In the reverse direction map each closed Kᵢ
into the new quotient by its inverse piece homeomorphism and the new canonical projection. Their
values agree on the whole chord because corresponding d,d⁻¹ parameters are identified in the new
quotient. The two-closed-set pasting theorem proves continuity on the full old disk;
continuousOn_iff_continuous_domRestrict and continuousOn_univ convert the exact restricted and total
interfaces. Assemble it with identity maps on unaffected face instances by the same coproduct
criterion.

Both assembled maps send every original edge-identification generator to equal quotient values,
including the retained parameters of every surviving side and the fresh chord identification.
EqvGen.rec propagates equality through reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity to the actual
generated setoid. Quotient.lift therefore defines each descended map, and the EXISTING
Continuous.quotient_lift terminal gives its continuity with no separation assumption on either
realization quotient. The lift computation rule and quotient equality on related representatives
verify the two composites on disk representatives; Quotient.ind then proves that both composites are
identity on the entire quotients. Thus the two descended maps are an actual homeomorphism preserving
all surviving boundary interval parameters. No compact-to-T2 shortcut is applied to an intermediate
edge quotient. The local compact-to-T2 use above concerns only a Euclidean disk. All of this step is
restricted to proper cuts/proper inverse merges and full-valence-two edge inverses: empty U or V and
entire-circle monogon donors remain outside THIS primitive conclusion. The exact nonempty-residual
reduction applications are supplied by the proper source compositions in Steps3/6, and the
both-empty isolated case is excluded by current dual connectivity and b≥1. No universal
null-boundary or improper-move theorem is asserted.

3. Use the actual written cancellation in Seifert–Threlfall §38 Step2 p140, Figures59–61, for a word
R a a⁻¹ S with BOTH R and S nonempty. Subdivide the parameter disk into the distinct faces R a d and
d⁻¹ a⁻¹ S, where d is fresh. Both sides of the cut are nonempty boundary intervals and the cut
endpoints are distinct positions in the abstract parameter circle. The corner between a and a⁻¹ was
the full valence-one spur vertex; after the cut it is the full valence-two vertex allowing the
source dimension-one inverse to merge a d to y and d⁻¹ a⁻¹ to y⁻¹. The faces become R y and y⁻¹ S.
Since R,S are nonempty, neither is a monogon whose entire circle is glued. Their distinct face
instances can be merged along the proper side y, leaving R S. Thus this written source replaces the
improper Gallier spur realization whenever there are at least two surviving side occurrences
distributed on its two sides. The operations preserve the interval identifications on all surviving
sides and use only proper cuts, full-valence-two edge inverses and distinct-face proper merges. This
is exactly the source p140 construction; it is not applied when the remaining string has only one
occurrence, nor advertised as a complete alternative boundary-classification algorithm.

4. CURRENT CHOSEN FINITE-SOURCE ROUTE: every nonempty spur endpoint uses the proper
Seifert–Threlfall cancellation when its residual has length≥2, and the exact one-P1/ST/inverse-P1
specialization when that length is1. Every monogon donor or empty-substring face operation used in
this reduction is replaced by the finite proper composition below or its reverse; the both-empty
isolated case is excluded. The original Gallier three-operation spur description is retained only as
the formal endpoint/corner calculation. The retained Lee folding proof is an OPTIONAL independent
endpoint homeomorphism and is NOT the selected finite P1/P2 factorization. No individual unproved
improper move is chosen in the current sequence. Each replacement has exactly the original macro
endpoint and is finite.

The proper-chord proof alone does not realize an empty-substring P2 or its inverse with an
entire-circle monogon donor. Gallier–Xu p86 leaves general realization invariance as an exercise,
and pp92–93 supply only the formal operations. Do NOT use a literal entire-circle-to-proper-edge
gluing map. For the exact nonempty-residual case used here, replace that formal operation by the
following finite composition of the ALREADY written and realized source moves: Gallier–Xu P1/p92
full-vertex inverse, its Proposition3.2+Remark p31/NoteF.4 p167 parameter-disk extensions, and
Seifert–Threlfall §37p138/§38Step2p140 proper cancellation.

Let the TWO DISTINCT donor face instances have cyclic words y and y⁻¹X, with X nonempty. The paired
label y has exactly these two occurrences; no third y occurrence is silently ignored. Apply global
P1 y=d e, using fresh labels and replacing y⁻¹ by e⁻¹d⁻¹ in the second donor. The donors become d e
and e⁻¹d⁻¹X. Each designated d occurrence is now a PROPER boundary arc: the first donor has the
nonempty remaining side e, and the other has remaining e⁻¹X, nonempty even before using X≠empty.
Merge these DISTINCT faces along d by the installed proper inverse P2. Its endpoint word is e X e⁻¹,
up to the chosen initial position in the same cyclic parameter circle. No monogon donor is glued by
its whole circle.

Before the merge the fresh y-subdivision midpoint zeta is the FULL incoming class {d,e⁻¹}; the two
original donor corners add only this same pair. Removing d leaves the FULL singleton {e⁻¹}. In e X
e⁻¹ this is exactly the fresh spur corner between e⁻¹ and e. All surviving original endpoint darts
and X corners remain outside zeta; in particular zeta is not the protected ORIGINAL root. Cyclically
write e⁻¹e X and delete ONLY this fresh spur by the source-supported cancellation already recorded
in the inner-elimination step. If |X|≥2, split its cyclic residual into two nonempty sides and use
the installed proper Seifert–Threlfall three-move sequence. If |X|1, use the approved ONE
residual-side P1/proper-ST/inverse-P1 five-move sequence. Both sequences retain every original
normalized X interval parameter. The resulting word is X. This supplies the exact nonempty-residual
endpoint of the formal monogon inverse P2 by a finite PROPER source composition, with the temporary
midpoint undone, and does not assert an individual exceptional gluing map.

The composite has zero net new vertex and removes one paired edge and one face, exactly the formal
donor-merge effect. P1 creates the fresh midpoint, the proper d merge removes its d incidence, and
cancellation deletes only that fresh point and the remaining e spur. Every X edge and every other
face map is unchanged. The actual disk, closed-pair/coproduct and generated-quotient realization
maps of the proper-move step compose, with identical parameters on paired surviving sides and
without any Hausdorff assumption on the full edge quotient. Reading this finite sequence backward
supplies the empty-substring P2 endpoint whenever its nonempty old boundary word is X. This is
source-move composition, not a new topology proof of all improper moves or a universal padding
theorem.

If X is empty, the donor words are y and y⁻¹ and these TWO faces exhaust both occurrences of their
only edge. They form an isolated two-face component of the CURRENT dual graph: neither donor has any
other label through which it could meet another face. Current dual connectivity therefore forces
them to be all faces. There would then be no unpaired boundary edge, contrary to the retained b≥1
boundary-contour invariant. Thus this both-empty operation is never an application in the present
reduction. This exclusion uses the existing given-edge/dual/boundary data, not a classification or
an unproved sphere homeomorphism.

The separately written moved-edge subdivision in the inner-slide step also supplies its exact
empty-residual slide endpoint by four proper/full-vertex moves. Consequently the former
one-surviving-side and empty-residual local realization notices are superseded ONLY by these
explicit source applications. The p86 exercise is not invoked as a proved general theorem. All prior
Lee/ST proper components are retained and the protected-root spur is never removed. Independent
review of the complete chosen finite composite now verifies all twelve steps at this node’s own
level, relative to its named prerequisites; no universal p86 exercise theorem or transitive Lean
closure is claimed.

5. At the level of finite words and their corner-relation vertices, P1 creates one subdivision
vertex and one additional edge and does not change the number of faces. P2 adds one paired label and
one face and does not change corner vertex classes: its two new d endpoints are exactly the two old
cut corners, even if those corners were globally identified. Thus Δ(V,E,F)=(1,1,0) for P1 and
(0,1,1) for P2, and the inverse moves have opposite changes; χ=V−E+F is invariant. The source
pp87–89 verifies these formal invariants, including coherent orientation and the number of boundary
contours. A coherent orientation is preserved by orienting the cut faces consistently and gluing
opposite designated occurrences; each surviving paired dart still occurs once with each sign. P1
subdivides one boundary cycle when its edge is unpaired; P2 adds only a paired edge and leaves the
unpaired contour walks unchanged. The CURRENT sequence chooses only realized proper/full-vertex
primitives or the explicit finite proper source compositions in Steps3/6/7. Therefore these
invariants apply to each primitive and to each completed macro, whose temporary vertices are undone.
Its realization and boundary correspondence follow from the actual source quotient homeomorphisms;
they are not inferred from the formal counts alone.

6. Retain an inner vertex α0. If the original simplicial complex has none, create one inside an
ORIGINAL triangle with boundary a1 a2 a3, using the explicit construction of Prop6.1 p86: split into
a1 d and d⁻¹ a2 a3, subdivide d=d1 d3⁻¹, and split the residual face d1⁻¹ a2 a3 d3 into d1⁻¹ a2 d2
and d2⁻¹ a3 d3. The three resulting words a1 d1 d3⁻¹, d1⁻¹ a2 d2, d2⁻¹ a3 d3 have central incoming
vertex (d1,d2,d3). These are proper cuts and one P1. From now on choose only inner vertices OTHER
THAN α0 for elimination. Do not globally delete newly formed spurs indiscriminately: in particular
the final disk word chc⁻¹ has a cyclic root spur c⁻¹c which must remain to keep the explicit
canonical inner vertex. The initial spur cleanup of Lemma6.1 is vacuous for the original simplicial
triangles, and this protected-root convention implements its instruction to retain one inner vertex.

7. Let α≠α0 be an inner incoming vertex. If every incoming dart in α had its inverse in α as well,
every edge incident to α would be a loop at α, isolating that vertex in the connected vertex-edge
graph. Since α0 is a different vertex, choose b1∈α with b1⁻¹ outside α. Its other endpoint need not
be α0 or even an inner vertex; no direct-inner-adjacency assumption is used. If α has at least two
entries, choose an adjacent inward dart b2 in its cyclic list. Then b2 differs from b1,b1⁻¹. Select
the boundary occurrence b1 b2⁻¹X1, split that face into the fresh triangle b1 b2⁻¹c and residual
c⁻¹X1, and locate the opposite occurrence b2. The triangle contains no b2, because b2 is different
from b1,b1⁻¹,c,c⁻¹. If both occurrences were in the same original face, b2 now occurs in the
residual face, which is a DISTINCT resulting face instance. If they were in different original
faces, that other face is still distinct. Hence the source P2⁻¹ gluing along b2 always uses distinct
faces, including repeated one-face occurrences. It gives the formal endpoint cb1X2 when the other
face is written b2X2. If X1 is nonempty but X2 is empty, that opposite donor is the monogon b2:
choose the Step3 P1/proper-merge/fresh-spur composition for this merge, with nonempty residual cb1,
instead of a literal whole-circle gluing. If both donors have proper designated arcs, use the
installed proper inverse P2. These choices have the SAME endpoint and surviving parameters. For X1
empty use the following exact source composition instead of that formal improper cut.

CURRENT EMPTY-RESIDUAL SOURCE APPLICATION. If X1 is empty, the selected face is b1 b2⁻¹. It contains
no positive b2, since b2 differs from b1 and b1⁻¹. Hence the other b2 occurrence MUST lie in another
original face instance; write that face b2X2, allowing X2 empty. Do not subdivide the anchor b1. Use
global P1 on the MOVED paired edge b2=s t with fresh labels, replacing b2⁻¹ by t⁻¹s⁻¹ in ALL
occurrences. The selected face becomes b1t⁻¹s⁻¹; the other is s t X2, written cyclically t X2s. The
fresh midpoint zeta is FULL {s,t⁻¹}; the original anchor/root endpoints remain unchanged.

Use the proper P2 cut into b1t⁻¹c and c⁻¹s⁻¹, with fresh c. The two cut substrings b1t⁻¹ and s⁻¹ are
nonempty, and the cut endpoints are distinct abstract-circle positions. Locate the opposite t in the
DISTINCT other face tX2s, and properly merge along t: the remaining strings c b1 and X2s are
nonempty even if X2 is empty. The result is c b1X2s together with the unchanged residual face
c⁻¹s⁻¹. This never merges a whole-circle donor; both designated t arcs are proper.

The cut adds c⁻¹ to the fresh class, giving {s,t⁻¹,c⁻¹}; the proper t merge removes t⁻¹, leaving
EXACTLY the FULL two-entry class {s,c⁻¹}. Thus only NOW is inverse P1 on the adjacent string s c
permitted. In the cyclic representative s c b1X2 replace s c by fresh y, and in the other face
replace c⁻¹s⁻¹ by y⁻¹. The final words are y b1X2 and y⁻¹, precisely the endpoint of the source's
formal empty-X1 slide with its fresh label renamed y. The monogon is created by a legitimate
FULL-valence-two EDGE inverse with the existing circular-disk/F.4 extension, not by an entire-circle
P2 merge.

The finite sequence is P1, P2, P2⁻¹, P1⁻¹. The source primitive interval maps preserve every
surviving b1 and X2 side pointwise, including all other paired and unpaired sides; the removed b2
has no surviving side parameter to preserve. The newly paired y,y⁻¹ use the same composite interval
parameter. The temporary midpoint is removed, so no new vertex PERSISTS. The old vertex-list effect
is exactly that of the original slide endpoint and the existing incoming-dart calculation below
still applies; the anchor b1 and protected old alpha0 are untouched. Original face instances and all
their other surviving maps are retained. Each geometric move uses the already written proper
