9. After inner reduction, an unfinished boundary vertex β has interval list (h,b1,…,bm,k), with
unpaired endpoint darts h,k and paired middle darts. The connected vertex-edge graph contains a
boundary vertex directly adjacent to α0. A completed loop vertex (h,c,h⁻¹) has only its spoke c to
α0 and the self-loop h, so it cannot be an intermediate vertex on a path from α0 to any other
vertex. Consequently, if unfinished boundary vertices remain, some unfinished β is directly adjacent
to α0 through a paired dart bi with bi⁻¹∈α0; an unpaired boundary edge has boundary vertices at both
endpoints and cannot connect to α0. Keep bi as anchor and apply the preceding slides to successive
paired neighbors on either side of it, reversing the interval order when needed. Each slide lowers
the number of middle entries by one and preserves h,k and the anchor’s outside endpoint, yielding
(h,bi,k). If h≠k⁻¹, then h⁻¹ is outside β: it cannot equal a paired middle dart or h, and it equals
k exactly in the excluded case. Use h as outside-endpoint anchor to slide out bi, leaving the FULL
vertex (h,k). P1⁻¹ can then merge the consecutive boundary edges h and k⁻¹, because their labels are
distinct and the full incoming vertex is (h,k); this deletes β. If h=k⁻¹, retain (h,c,h⁻¹) as a
finished loop. Its corner relations give a consecutive word chc⁻¹; the remaining spoke dart c⁻¹
leads to α0. Future reductions at a different boundary vertex cannot use h,h⁻¹,c, which all lead to
this completed vertex, nor c⁻¹, which leads to α0. Therefore the finished block is untouched. Each
local slide decreases a finite list length; completing or deleting a boundary vertex decreases the
number of unfinished boundary vertices, with no new vertex PERSISTING at a completed source-macro
endpoint; the temporary subdivision vertices in the chosen proper compositions are fully undone.
Finitely many iterations leave exactly one loop vertex per boundary contour, hence b loop blocks and
the single protected inner vertex.

10. While at least two face instances remain, dual connectivity provides a paired edge joining
distinct instances. If both designated occurrences are proper arcs, apply the installed proper P2⁻¹.
If one donor is a monogon, use the exact P1/proper-merge/fresh-spur-cancellation source composition
of the exceptional-move step, whose net effect is the SAME merged residual word and one fewer face.
If both donors are monogons, the isolated two-face dual component would exhaust the complex and have
no unpaired boundary edge, excluded by current dual connectivity and b≥1. Thus every actual merge
here has a source-supported finite proper realization and the face count drops by one. Corner vertex
lists and already finished loop blocks persist. This gives one face in finitely many moves.
Coherence implies that every paired label in this face has one positive and one inverse occurrence;
crosscap operations and handle-to-crosscap conversions in the general source proof are unnecessary.
For an interlaced pair use the written slide rule aUVa⁻¹X≃dVUd⁻¹X: split into aUc and c⁻¹Va⁻¹X and
paste their distinct face instances along a, relabeling d=c⁻¹. Applying this three times to
aUbVa⁻¹Xb⁻¹Y produces a2 b1 a2⁻¹ b1⁻¹ YXVU, as in Lemma6.1 Step4 pp94–95. Thus each extraction gives
an adjacent handle block. Existing handle and loop blocks are passed as WHOLE substrings: their
labels are disjoint from the two chosen unused labels, so every cut made at an unused occurrence
lies outside them. Each extraction removes two paired labels from the unused set, proving finite
termination. Empty U or V may be permitted in the formal rules, but the two cut strings aU and Va⁻¹X
are nonempty because they contain the indicated occurrences of a and a⁻¹; hence these handle cuts
themselves are proper.

11. Suppose an unused paired label c remains after all interlaced unused pairs have been extracted.
Write the unique face word cXc⁻¹Y. No unused paired label has one occurrence in X and one in Y, or
it would interlace c and give another handle. Every completed handle or loop block lies wholly in X
or wholly in Y, since c is not one of its labels. Therefore no edge label in X equals or is the
inverse of a label in Y. Define the signed subset S={c}∪{x,x⁻¹ : a label of x occurs in X}; this
includes both darts of a boundary edge if it occurs in X. Then c⁻¹∉S. Every corner relation u~v⁻¹
lies entirely in S or entirely in its complement: the corner after c and before the first letter of
X stays in S, the corner between the last letter of X and c⁻¹ ends at its INVERSE c∈S, and all
internal X corners stay in S. If X is empty, the corner cc⁻¹ gives the self-relation c~c. The
analogous Y corners stay in the complement and the closing corner ends at c⁻¹. Thus the entire
incoming vertex component of c is contained in S and excludes c⁻¹. But neither dart of the unused c
can lead to a completed boundary vertex, whose only paired incoming dart is its already-used spoke.
Both must therefore lead to the unique inner vertex α0. This contradicts their separation into
different corner components. This is the exact finite successor-walk exhaustion in source Step5 p95,
with incoming-neighbor inversion corrected; it does not assert that c⁻¹ is both visited and
unvisited. No additional graph or classification theorem is assumed.

12. The one-face word is now a concatenation of handle blocks and b loop blocks. Source Step6 p95
uses the same slide rule to bring loop blocks adjacent: chc⁻¹Xdkd⁻¹Y≃c1hc1⁻¹dkd⁻¹YX, carrying all
intervening handle blocks as whole substrings. Repetition groups every loop, and a cyclic rotation
places the remaining handle blocks first. Relabeling gives exactly ∏_(i=1)^g ai bi ai⁻¹ bi⁻¹
∏_(j=1)^b cj hj cj⁻¹, with g≥0. Its corner graph has the b boundary vertices (hj,cj,hj⁻¹) and one
inner vertex, hence V=b+1, E=2g+2b and F=1; the preserved finite count gives V(K)−E(K)+F(K)=2−2g−b.
This works for g=0,b=1 (word chc⁻¹, V2,E2,F1), for g=0,b=2 (two loop blocks, V3,E4,F1), and for
arbitrary b≥1 without the null-sphere convention. The formal sequence and count are justified by the
source combinatorial proof above. The CURRENT finite sequence explicitly chooses the realized proper
P1/P2 primitives and full-vertex inverses, replacing every exceptional reduction application by the
finite proper source compositions in Steps3/6/7. Compose the original triangle-quotient
homeomorphism with these actual elementary quotient homeomorphisms. This gives the homeomorphism
demanded in the statement, with the same normalized interval parameters on every surviving side, and
carries the b preserved boundary contours bijectively to the final unpaired hj loops. Each local
macro is finite, and the stated endpoint list/vertex/face/unused-label measures make the complete
sequence finite. Thus the geometric conclusion and Euler formula are proved at this node’s own
level, relative to the named prerequisites. No general p86 exercise theorem, classification
shortcut, or transitive Lean completion is claimed.

-/
theorem orientable_triangle_presentation_reduction
    {N b : ℕ} (K : AbstractSimplicialComplex (Fin N)) (hb : 1 ≤ b)
    (hpure : PureTwo K) (hconnected : IsConnected (carrier K))
    (hincidence : ∀ e ∈ simplices K 1, incidence K e = 1 ∨ incidence K e = 2)
    (hlinks : SurfaceLinks K) (B : Fin b → List (Fin N)) (hboundary : BoundaryCycles K B)
    (o : (s : Triangle K) → Fin 3 ≃ ↥s.val)
    (horiented : Oriented (trianglePresentation K o))
    (H₀ : ↥(carrier K) ≃ₜ Realization (trianglePresentation K o))
    (hH₀ : OriginalParameters K o H₀) :
    ∃ (g : ℕ) (Q : Presentation) (e : ℕ ≃ ℕ) (flip : ℕ → Bool)
      (f : Fin Q.length ≃ Fin ([normalWord g b] : Presentation).length),
      (∀ k, ((Q.get k).map fun a ↦ (e a.1, if flip a.1 then !a.2 else a.2)) =
        ([normalWord g b] : Presentation).get (f k)) ∧
      ∃ sequence : RealizedSequence (trianglePresentation K o) Q,
        (∃ σ : Fin b ≃ Fin b, ∀ j : Fin b,
          (H₀.trans sequence.homeomorph) '' cycleCarrier (K := K) (B j) =
            edgeImage Q (e.symm (2 * g + 2 * (σ j).val + 1))) ∧
        ((simplices K 0).card : ℤ) - ((simplices K 1).card : ℤ) +
          ((simplices K 2).card : ℤ) =
          (2 : ℤ) - 2 * (g : ℤ) - (b : ℤ) := by
  obtain ⟨w, _⟩ := orientable_triangle_presentation_reduction_witness K hb hpure hconnected
    hincidence hlinks B hboundary o horiented H₀ hH₀
  exact ⟨w.g, w.Q, w.e, w.flip, w.f, w.wordEq, w.sequence, ⟨w.sigma, w.boundaryEq⟩, w.eulerEq⟩

end HolomorphicAnosov.Topology.GallierXu
